Eye Conditions Treated at Radhakrishna Netralay

The right treatment depends on the underlying condition, its severity, and the patient's overall eye health. Specialist evaluation can help identify the cause of symptoms and determine the appropriate course of management.

Refractive Errors

Refractive errors occur when light does not focus correctly on the retina. Common problems include:

Myopia or short-sightedness
Hyperopia or long-sightedness
Astigmatism
Presbyopia or age-related difficulty with near vision

Treatment may include spectacles, contact lenses, or refractive surgery for suitable candidates.

Cataract

A cataract causes the natural lens of the eye to become cloudy and can gradually reduce vision.

Radhakrishna Netralay provides evaluation and surgical management for cataract, including modern cataract surgery and intraocular lens implantation where appropriate.

Glaucoma

Glaucoma is a group of eye conditions that can damage the optic nerve. It may progress without obvious symptoms in its early stages.

Depending on the type and severity, glaucoma management may involve:

Prescription eye drops
Regular monitoring
OCT and visual-field testing
Laser procedures
Surgical treatment when indicated

Early diagnosis is particularly important because optic-nerve damage caused by glaucoma cannot generally be reversed.

Retina Disorders

Retinal diseases can affect central or peripheral vision and may require specialist management.

The hospital provides evaluation and treatment for conditions including:

Diabetic retinopathy
Retinal detachment
Age-related macular degeneration
Macular disorders
Epiretinal membrane
Macular hole
Retinal vascular conditions

Depending on the diagnosis, treatment may include retinal laser, intravitreal injections, or vitreoretinal surgery.

Corneal and Anterior Segment Conditions

The cornea is the transparent front surface of the eye and plays an important role in focusing light.

Eye specialists can evaluate conditions such as:

Corneal infections
Corneal ulcers
Pterygium
Dry eye disease
Conjunctivitis
Allergic eye conditions
Other anterior-segment disorders
Pediatric Eye Conditions

Children need age-appropriate eye examinations because undetected vision problems can interfere with normal visual development.

Pediatric eye services may include evaluation and management of:

Squint or strabismus
Amblyopia or lazy eye
Childhood refractive errors
Congenital eye conditions
Other problems affecting visual development
Oculoplasty and Eyelid Conditions

Oculoplasty involves conditions affecting the eyelids, orbit, and surrounding structures.

Depending on the diagnosis, patients may require evaluation for conditions such as ptosis, entropion, ectropion, or other eyelid abnormalities.

What Happens During an Eye Specialist Consultation?

A comprehensive eye consultation is more than simply checking whether you can read letters from a distance.

Your examination may include:

1. Patient History

The ophthalmologist discusses your symptoms, previous eye problems, medical history, medications, and relevant family history.

Conditions such as diabetes and hypertension can be particularly important when assessing eye health.

2. Vision Assessment

Distance and near vision are checked, with or without your current glasses or contact lenses.

3. Refraction

If required, refraction is performed to determine the appropriate spectacle or contact-lens prescription.

4. Slit-Lamp Examination

A slit lamp provides a magnified examination of structures such as the cornea, conjunctiva, iris, and lens.

5. Dilated Eye Examination

Dilating drops may be used when the doctor needs a detailed view of the retina and optic nerve.

6. Additional Diagnostic Tests

Depending on your symptoms and examination findings, the ophthalmologist may recommend tests such as:

OCT
Visual-field testing
Corneal topography
Retinal imaging
Biometry
Eye-pressure measurement

Not every patient requires every test. The ophthalmologist determines which investigations are clinically appropriate.

7. Diagnosis and Treatment Plan

After reviewing the examination and test results, the doctor explains the diagnosis and discusses appropriate treatment or monitoring options.

When Should You See an Eye Specialist in Kalyan?

Some eye symptoms should not be ignored.

Seek prompt ophthalmic evaluation if you experience:

Sudden loss or significant reduction of vision
Sudden onset of flashes or many new floaters
A curtain or shadow across your vision
Significant or persistent eye pain
Redness associated with pain or light sensitivity
Double vision
Eye injury or suspected foreign body
Sudden changes in your field of vision

These symptoms can sometimes indicate conditions that require urgent treatment.

Even without symptoms, regular eye examinations become increasingly important with age and for people with conditions such as diabetes, hypertension, or a family history of glaucoma.

Eye Care for Diabetic Patients

Diabetes can affect the small blood vessels supplying the retina and may lead to diabetic retinopathy.

Importantly, diabetic eye disease can develop before noticeable changes in vision occur. Regular retinal examinations can therefore help identify changes earlier.

Depending on the stage of diabetic retinopathy, treatment may include monitoring, retinal laser, injections, or surgery.

If you have diabetes, ask your ophthalmologist how frequently you should have your eyes examined.

